  biography = $$Emmanuel Babled is a French-Italian designer whose practice sits at the rare intersection of contemporary design, sculpture, and the master ateliers of Murano and Carrara. Born in France in 1967 and trained at the Scuola Politecnica di Design in Milan, he settled in Italy in the late 1980s and has spent the past three decades pushing glass, marble, ceramic and bronze into forms that feel at once primordial and futuristic.

His early collaborations with the historic Murano furnaces — Venini, Salviati, and the maestros of Murano — established him as one of the few outside designers fluent enough in the language of blown glass to be entrusted with experimental, technically uncompromising pieces. From those workshops emerged the vocabulary that defines his work today: pure geometric volumes, weighted asymmetry, and a deep respect for the gesture of the maker.

The Osmosi Series — sculptural objects in which solid Carrara marble is digitally carved and then fused with hand-blown Murano glass — is the most celebrated expression of this philosophy. Each piece is conceived as a dialogue between two opposing materials and two opposing methods: the algorithmic precision of CNC milling and the ancient, breath-driven craft of the glassblower. The result is a body of work that has become a reference for collectors of contemporary décoratif.

Babled's pieces are held in the permanent collections of the Centre Pompidou in Paris, the Stedelijk Museum in Amsterdam, the MUDAC in Lausanne, and the Corning Museum of Glass in New York. He has exhibited at the Venice Biennale, Design Miami, PAD Paris and London, and Nilufar Gallery, and his limited editions are commissioned by an international circle of private collectors and interior architects.

Working today between Lisbon and Italy, Babled continues to operate as both designer and editor of his own pieces — each one signed, numbered, and produced in tightly limited series by the artisans he has worked with for a lifetime.$$
